$ and $\\mathbb{E}[u(Y)]$.","$u(w-\\mathbb{E}[X])$ and $u(w-\\mathbb{E}[Y])$.","$\\mathbb{E}[u(w+X)]$ and $\\mathbb{E}[u(w+Y)]$.","$\\mathbb{E}[u(w-X)]$ and $\\mathbb{E}[u(w-Y)]$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"bb28167f9cfb2a842a8d4eb4","time_limit":35,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_2_2b_risk_insurance_second_order_stochastic_dominance_c57d068b_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"A gain Y is certainly one, with CDF zero for $0\\le s<1$ and one for $s\\ge1$. What is the integrated CDF area $I_Y(1)=\\int_0^1 F_Y(s)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$, despite the endpoint value $F_Y(1)=1$?","options":["$0$.","$1$.","$1/2$.","It is undefined because the CDF jumps at one."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"02aece79ffa82c2d3b2d8e61","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_2_2b_risk_insurance_second_order_stochastic_dominance_c57d068b_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Gain X is one or nine with equal probabilities, while Y is certainly five. For increasing concave utility $u(w)=\\log w$ on positive wealth, which expected-utility comparison is correct?","options":["$\\mathbb{E}[u(X)]=\\log5=\\mathbb{E}[u(Y)]$.","$\\mathbb{E}[u(X)]=\\log3<\\log5=\\mathbb{E}[u(Y)]$.","$\\mathbb{E}[u(X)]=\\log9>\\log5=\\mathbb{E}[u(Y)]$.","$\\mathbb{E}[u(X)]=\\log3>\\log5=\\mathbb{E}[u(Y)]$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"16dcbd5f583498ed6f169c46","time_limit":25,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_2_2b_risk_insurance_second_order_stochastic_dominance_c57d068b_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Non-negative gains X,Y are both at most four. At threshold five their integr